WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
The document is open.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.580
At the top it says

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.400
Q3 Innovation Plan.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.660
The cursor blinks, your inbox

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.460
is full of requests, your

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.200
team wants a direction, and

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.720
somebody wants a polished plan

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.440
by Friday.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.700
I know that room.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.500
The pressure makes the blank

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.320
page feel like the problem.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.220
So you open a tool,

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.300
ask for ideas, and in

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.860
30 seconds you have a

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.420
confident looking answer.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:25.420
Now what?

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.500
Who will decide which idea

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.060
matters.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.030
What evidence would make that

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:30.910
choice responsible?

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.240
What data did you put

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.140
into the tool to get

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:35.560
there?

00:00:37.763 --> 00:00:39.183
A beautiful page can hide

00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:40.263
those questions for a little

00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:40.743
while.

00:00:42.807 --> 00:00:44.107
It cannot answer them.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.770
In the opening of Accelerated,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:48.910
I start with that blinking

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.470
cursor because it is familiar.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.040
The book begins with product

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.080
teams carrying noise, conflicting

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.360
priorities, and documents that do

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.520
not move decisions.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.090
The work here is to

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:03.930
give the team a way

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.230
to learn, choose, and keep

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.410
its judgment when the page

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:07.930
fills up.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.370
A product system sounds larger

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.490
than it needs to be.

00:01:13.814 --> 00:01:14.974
Think about the last idea

00:01:14.994 --> 00:01:16.114
that arrived in a meeting.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:19.497
Someone noticed a customer

00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:20.077
problem.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.281
Someone else had a competing

00:01:23.301 --> 00:01:23.881
story.

00:01:26.064 --> 00:01:27.384
A third person asked for

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:28.764
a number nobody had.

00:01:30.908 --> 00:01:32.028
The team left with another

00:01:32.048 --> 00:01:33.048
document to write.

00:01:35.211 --> 00:01:36.531
A system gives those moments

00:01:36.551 --> 00:01:37.191
a path.

00:01:39.394 --> 00:01:40.374
It helps you record the

00:01:40.394 --> 00:01:42.814
signal, name the decision, state

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:44.214
what you believe, and come

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.054
back to what happened.

00:01:45.074 --> 00:01:47.314
AI can help organize messy

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:48.994
input, test the shape of

00:01:49.014 --> 00:01:50.274
an argument, and make a

00:01:50.294 --> 00:01:51.014
first draft.

00:01:53.344 --> 00:01:54.824
The judgment still belongs to

00:01:54.844 --> 00:01:55.964
people who understand the

00:01:55.984 --> 00:01:56.604
consequences.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:01:59.968
That is the argument in

00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:00.848
the first chapter.

00:02:02.991 --> 00:02:04.371
I describe AI as a

00:02:04.391 --> 00:02:05.971
strategy partner, a signal

00:02:05.991 --> 00:02:07.551
amplifier, and a structure

00:02:07.571 --> 00:02:08.031
builder.

00:02:10.355 --> 00:02:11.295
Those are jobs it may

00:02:11.315 --> 00:02:12.555
help with, under the right

00:02:12.575 --> 00:02:13.195
conditions.

00:02:15.525 --> 00:02:16.865
They are not permission to

00:02:16.885 --> 00:02:18.225
trust every output.

00:02:20.488 --> 00:02:21.408
If it gives you five

00:02:21.428 --> 00:02:23.128
polished options, you still need

00:02:23.148 --> 00:02:24.768
to ask who benefits, what

00:02:24.788 --> 00:02:26.248
is missing and which option

00:02:26.268 --> 00:02:27.388
you can test safely.

00:02:29.718 --> 00:02:30.658
Speed can create room to

00:02:30.678 --> 00:02:31.058
think.

00:02:33.142 --> 00:02:34.082
It can also fill that

00:02:34.102 --> 00:02:35.222
room with more noise.

00:02:37.385 --> 00:02:38.125
Look at this map.

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:40.889
Signals.

00:02:43.172 --> 00:02:43.812
Alignment.

00:02:46.096 --> 00:02:46.736
Momentum.

00:02:48.899 --> 00:02:49.539
It is a way to

00:02:49.559 --> 00:02:50.639
navigate the workshop.

00:02:52.942 --> 00:02:54.602
First, take the raw material

00:02:54.622 --> 00:02:55.722
coming at you and find

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.042
a question worth answering.

00:02:59.206 --> 00:03:00.486
Then give the people carrying

00:03:00.506 --> 00:03:02.326
the decision a shared direction.

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:05.569
Then build a rhythm that

00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:06.589
lets the next round of

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:07.869
evidence change the plan.

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:11.759
The book organizes its printed

00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:14.559
chapters as systems, align, and

00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:15.099
momentum.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:18.789
The workshop uses the three

00:03:18.809 --> 00:03:20.069
labels on this existing

00:03:20.089 --> 00:03:21.929
infographic as a visual guide.

00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:25.153
You will see where those

00:03:25.173 --> 00:03:26.013
paths cross.

00:03:28.316 --> 00:03:29.296
The graphic is not a

00:03:29.316 --> 00:03:30.536
replacement for the chapters.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.040
In these first lessons, we

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.160
stay close to the signal

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:35.640
side.

00:03:37.943 --> 00:03:39.523
We will choose one decision,

00:03:39.663 --> 00:03:41.443
work backward to evidence, and

00:03:41.463 --> 00:03:42.623
ask what a small test

00:03:42.643 --> 00:03:43.503
could teach us.

00:03:45.747 --> 00:03:46.767
There is a human thread

00:03:46.787 --> 00:03:47.787
through the book, too.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.690
Sam, a product manager who

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:52.990
has talent but keeps getting

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.550
pulled into reactive work.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:56.660
That story makes the pressure

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.220
visible.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.360
It is a teaching story

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.000
from the book, not a

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.680
claim about a current customer.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.980
Keep that distinction in view

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.860
as we move between story,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.620
worksheet, and public proof.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.030
Before you try any prompt,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.970
decide what information can leave

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:12.590
your desk.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.121
The book's data safety note

00:04:16.141 --> 00:04:17.561
comes before the chapters for

00:04:17.581 --> 00:04:18.141
a reason.

00:04:20.471 --> 00:04:22.191
Customer details, finances,

00:04:22.711 --> 00:04:24.531
internal strategy, and company

00:04:24.551 --> 00:04:26.451
records can carry obligations that

00:04:26.471 --> 00:04:27.951
a convenient text box does

00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:28.771
not erase.

00:04:31.043 --> 00:04:32.103
My working rule for this

00:04:32.123 --> 00:04:33.003
course is simple.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.114
Use public information, material

00:04:37.134 --> 00:04:38.754
your organization has approved for

00:04:38.774 --> 00:04:40.194
that use, or a made

00:04:40.214 --> 00:04:40.914
up example.

00:04:43.065 --> 00:04:44.725
If you are unsure, stop

00:04:44.745 --> 00:04:46.005
and check the policy that

00:04:46.025 --> 00:04:46.925
governs your work.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:50.595
Do not put restricted material

00:04:50.615 --> 00:04:52.055
into an external model to

00:04:52.075 --> 00:04:53.595
make the worksheet feel realistic.

00:04:55.887 --> 00:04:56.747
We can learn the method

00:04:56.767 --> 00:04:57.947
with a synthetic case.

00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:01.358
Imagine a team trying to

00:05:01.378 --> 00:05:02.678
improve the first week for

00:05:02.698 --> 00:05:03.338
new members.

00:05:05.668 --> 00:05:07.028
We can invent a harmless

00:05:07.048 --> 00:05:08.588
request list and test our

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:10.108
reasoning without using anyone's

00:05:10.128 --> 00:05:12.308
account, message, or history.

00:05:14.559 --> 00:05:16.039
On the screen, watch the

00:05:16.059 --> 00:05:17.439
example move into a three

00:05:17.459 --> 00:05:18.259
column boundary.

00:05:18.279 --> 00:05:20.499
public, approved internal,

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:21.239
restricted.

00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:25.429
Approved internal means approved

00:05:25.449 --> 00:05:27.069
for this particular tool and

00:05:27.089 --> 00:05:29.029
purpose, not merely available to

00:05:29.049 --> 00:05:29.669
you at work.

00:05:31.999 --> 00:05:33.699
This boundary follows the decision

00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:35.339
file through every lesson.

00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:38.531
It is part of product

00:05:38.551 --> 00:05:40.131
judgment, not an appendix.

00:05:42.461 --> 00:05:43.461
Let's make the first page

00:05:43.481 --> 00:05:44.581
of your decision file.
